.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL_FORK_PREPARE 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm OPENSSL_fork_prepare ,
.Nm OPENSSL_fork_parent ,
.Nm OPENSSL_fork_child
.Nd OpenSSL fork handlers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/crypto.h
The following functions have been deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0, and can
be hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft void
.Fo OPENSSL_fork_prepare
.Fa void
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OPENSSL_fork_parent
.Fa void
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OPENSSL_fork_child
.Fa void
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These methods are currently unused, and as such, no replacement methods
are required or planned.
.Pp
OpenSSL has state that should be reset when a process forks.
For example, the entropy pool used to generate random numbers (and
therefore encryption keys) should not be shared across multiple
programs.
The
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_prepare ,
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_parent ,
and
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_child
functions are used to reset this internal state.
.Pp
Platforms without fork(2) will probably not need to use these functions.
Platforms with fork(2) but without pthread_atfork(3) will probably need
to call them manually, as described in the following paragraph.
Platforms such as Linux that have both functions will normally not need
to call these functions as the OpenSSL library will do so automatically.
.Pp
.Xr OPENSSL_init_crypto 3
will register these functions with the appropriate handler, when the
.Sy OPENSSL_INIT_ATFORK
flag is used.
For other applications, these functions can be called directly.
They should be used according to the calling sequence described by the
pthread_atfork(3) documentation, which is summarized here.
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_prepare
should be called before a
.Xr fork 3
is done.
After the
.Xr fork 3
returns, the parent process should call
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_parent
and the child process should call
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_child .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_prepare ,
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_parent
and
.Fn OPENSSL_fork_child
do not return values.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr OPENSSL_init_crypto 3
.Sh HISTORY
These functions were added in OpenSSL 1.1.1.
